WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:17.129
Picture a ladder against a wall. Its length comes from how far out the foot is, and how high it reaches. That is Pythagoras. A vector works the same way. The length of a vector is called its magnitude.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:27.874
For a vector with i part x and j part y, the magnitude is the square root of x squared plus y squared. We write the magnitude of v with two straight lines, like this.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:41.723
To avoid a square root, work with magnitude squared. Magnitude squared is x squared plus y squared. If the length is given, set this equal to the length squared, and an unknown inside the vector can be found.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:51.805
This question type gives a length and asks for an unknown such as p. Often there are two possible values, because squaring loses the sign.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:01:04.916
Step one: write the vector in components, subtracting position vectors if needed. Step two: write magnitude squared as the sum of the squares of the components.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:17.506
Step three: set it equal to the given length squared. Step four: expand and solve, often a quadratic. Then use any condition, or give all possible values.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:25.450
The trap is forgetting to square the given length. If the length is three root ten, the right side is ninety, not three root ten.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:44.756
January twenty twenty. Vector A B is seven i plus p j. Vector A D is four i minus two p j. The length of B D is three root ten. Find the possible values of p. Three marks.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:57.422
First, vector B D. End minus start: vector A D minus vector A B. Four i minus two p j, minus, seven i plus p j, is minus three i minus three p j.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:06.149
Magnitude squared is the sum of the squares. Minus three, squared, is nine. Minus three p, squared, is nine p squared.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:15.897
This equals the given length squared. Three root ten, squared, is nine times ten, which is ninety. So nine plus nine p squared equals ninety.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:27.427
Nine p squared is eighty one, so p squared is nine. Then p is three, or minus three. Both give the same length, because squaring loses the sign.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:42.287
A tempting wrong route forgets to square the right side: nine plus nine p squared equals three root ten. That gives p of about nought point two three, and the check fails, because the length of B D would be only about three.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:49.240
So p is three or minus three. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:03:04.130
A common mistake is squaring badly. Minus three p, squared, is nine p squared, not minus nine p squared. The wrong sign gives p squared equals minus nine, which is impossible.

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:25.671
Now you try, from June twenty twenty three. Vector O A is five i plus seven j. Vector A B is a i plus sixteen j. The length of O B is five root twenty nine. Find the possible values of a. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:39.137
First vector O B: O A plus A B, which is five plus a in the i part, and twenty three in the j part. Magnitude squared equals the length squared, twenty five times twenty nine, which is seven hundred and twenty five.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:53.821
Take away five twenty nine: five plus a, squared, is one ninety six. Take the square root, positive and negative. Five plus a is fourteen, or minus fourteen. So a is nine, or minus nineteen.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:01.833
The most common wrong answer gives only a equals nine. The question says possible values, so you need both roots.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:08.551
Remember: find the vector, square its components, set equal to the length squared, and solve.
